---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { DMG, LINUX_SHA256_AARCH64, LINUX_SHA256_X86_64, VERSION, linuxTarball } from '../site';

const toc = [
  ['install', 'Install'],
  ['sessions', 'Sessions and tabs'],
  ['navigate', 'Get around'],
  ['remote', 'Remote hosts'],
  ['server-install', 'Install on a server yourself'],
  ['scrollback', 'Scrollback'],
  ['swarmctl', 'swarmctl'],
  ['daemon', 'The daemon'],
  ['troubleshooting', 'Troubleshooting'],
];
---

<Base title="Swarm docs: install, persistent sessions, SSH hosts and swarmctl" description="How to install Swarm on macOS, keep terminal sessions running after you close the app, attach to sessions on remote servers over SSH, and script them with swarmctl.">
  <div class="frame docs">
    <aside>
      <p class="label"># docs <span class="dim">[ {VERSION} ]</span></p>
      <nav aria-label="Docs">
        {toc.map(([id, title]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a>)}
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <article class="prose">
      <h1>Swarm docs</h1>
      <p class="lede">Everything you need to run Swarm: the app, its daemon, and the command line.</p>

      <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><a href={DMG}>Download Swarm-{VERSION}.dmg</a>. It's a universal app for Apple silicon and Intel and needs macOS 13 Ventura or later.</li>
        <li>Open the DMG and drag <strong>Swarm.app</strong> to Applications.</li>
        <li>Open Swarm. It's notarized by Apple, so macOS opens it after the usual "downloaded from the internet" question.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Or install it with Homebrew, which also puts <code>swarmctl</code> on your <code>PATH</code>:</p>
      <pre class="term">brew install --cask arturborycki/swarm/swarm</pre>
      <p>
        Swarm checks for new versions shortly after launch and daily (also <strong>Swarm → Check for Updates…</strong>)
        and offers <strong>Install and Restart</strong>. It installs only downloads signed with Swarm's release key, and
        your sessions keep running through the restart.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Settings → About, <strong>Download updates in the background</strong> has Swarm download a new version first and
        then offer to restart into it; if you choose <strong>Later</strong>, quitting Swarm installs it. With{' '}
        <strong>Install updates while Swarm isn't in use</strong> also on, Swarm installs it once you've been in another
        app for a minute and reopens in the background.
      </p>
      <p>If you installed from the DMG, link <code>swarmctl</code> onto your <code>PATH</code>:</p>
      <pre class="term">ln -s /Applications/Swarm.app/Contents/MacOS/swarmctl /usr/local/bin/swarmctl</pre>

      <h2 id="sessions">Sessions and tabs</h2>
      <p>
        Every tab is a <em>session</em>: a shell owned by Swarm's daemon, <code>swarmd</code>, not by the window.
        Closing a tab detaches it; the session keeps running and waits in the sidebar's <strong>detached</strong> list
        until you reopen it. To end a session for good, choose <strong>Terminate</strong> from its menu, or run{' '}
        <code>swarmctl rm</code>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Quitting the app leaves every session running. When it starts again, it reopens the tabs you had open.</li>
        <li>Reopened tabs show their earlier output, including blocks, exit codes and durations.</li>
        <li>Splitting a pane starts a new session, so each pane can be detached and reattached on its own.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="groups-splits">Groups and splits</h3>
      <p>
        The sidebar keeps sessions in groups: <strong>This Mac</strong> for local sessions, one group per remote host,
        and any groups you make yourself. Groups collapse, drag, and have their own menu; new local sessions go to This
        Mac unless the session you're on is in a group of your own.
      </p>
      <p>
        To see sessions side by side, right-click one in the sidebar and choose <strong>Open in Split</strong> (right,
        left, down or up), or drag it onto the open session: the pane under the pointer is highlighted on the side it
        will take, and dropping near the outer edge takes that whole side. Sessions from different groups, local and
        remote, can share one window, and they keep running as they move. Drag the dividers to resize.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Rename Pane…</strong> names just the focused pane's session; renaming a tab names all of its sessions.
        The Session menu also moves panes and tabs around: <strong>Move Pane to New Tab</strong>,{' '}
        <strong>Move Pane to Tab…</strong> (choose the tab in Go to Anything), <strong>Move Pane to New Window</strong>{' '}
        and <strong>Move Tab to New Window</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>View → Import Theme…</strong> turns an iTerm2 color preset (<code>.itermcolors</code>) or a Ghostty theme
        file into a Swarm theme and switches to it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="navigate">Get around</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Go to Anything</strong> (⌘P, or File → Go to Anything…) finds whatever you type among open tabs,
        detached sessions and hosts. Sessions whose program reports something come first, with its message ("Needs you:
        Run npm install?"), so typing <em>needs you</em> takes you to the session that is waiting. It also offers a new
        session in each folder your sessions use, on each host, or in a folder path you type. The command palette is on
        ⇧⌘P.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Activity</strong> (⌘., or the ACTIVITY toggle at the top of the sidebar) narrows the sidebar to
          sessions that need you, finished, failed, report work or have unread output, most urgent first within each
          group, and sums them up in its header.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Filter Sessions…</strong> (⌥⌘F) finds sessions by name, host or state, such as "needs you", "finished"
          or "muted". <strong>Navigate Back</strong> and <strong>Forward</strong> (⌃⌘[ and ⌃⌘]) step through the sessions
          you were in.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scratch Terminal</strong> (⌥⌘T, or View → Scratch Terminal) shows or hides a floating terminal over
          every window. Drag it by its top edge to move it. Its tabs are sessions like any others, so they keep running
          while it's hidden; Settings → Features sets its position and size.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Copy Mode</strong> (⇧⌘X) puts a cursor in the scrollback and moves it as in vi: hjkl or the arrows,
          w b e by word, 0 ^ $ along the line, g and G to the top and bottom, Ctrl-U and Ctrl-D by half a screen, with a
          count such as 5j. Start a selection with v (V for whole lines, o to jump to its other end), copy with y or
          Return, leave with Esc. / opens Find; n and N then move the cursor between its matches.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Go to Directory…</strong> (⇧⌘G) lists the folders your sessions use on the same host; choosing one
          runs <code>cd</code> there, or opens a new session in it if a program is running.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Find in All Sessions…</strong> (⇧⌘F) searches the output of every session, on this Mac and on
          connected hosts, detached ones and output kept on disk included. Type the text after <em>find</em> in Go to
          Anything and press Return; the matching lines are listed, newest first, with their session. Choosing one opens
          that session with Find on the text. It matches plain text, ignoring case unless you type a capital letter.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>tmux-style prefix keys</strong>: in Settings → Keyboard Shortcuts, choose Ctrl-B (as in tmux) or
          Ctrl-A (as in screen). Then the prefix and a key work on panes and tabs: <code>c</code> new tab,{' '}
          <code>%</code> split right, <code>"</code> split down, the arrows move between panes, <code>o</code> next pane,{' '}
          <code>z</code> zoom, <code>x</code> close the pane, <code>n</code> and <code>p</code> next and previous tab,{' '}
          <code>1</code>–<code>9</code> a tab, <code>,</code> rename the pane, <code>[</code> Copy Mode, <code>w</code>{' '}
          Go to Anything. <code>r</code> starts resize mode: the arrows (or hjkl) resize the pane until Esc or Return.
          The prefix pressed twice passes it on: to the running program, such as a full-screen editor, or at the
          prompt to the input (Ctrl-A to the start of the line).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Insert Path…</strong> (⇧⌘I) types the paths of files or folders you choose into the session. In a
          session on another host, these files, and any you drop on it, are copied there first (to{' '}
          <code>~/.cache/swarm/uploads/</code>) and their paths there are typed, so you can hand a screenshot to an agent
          running on a server.{' '}
          <strong>Quick Look Selection</strong> (⇧⌘Y) previews a selected path, or opens a selected link in the browser.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A background session that needs you, finishes or fails sends a notification, and sessions waiting for you are
        counted on the Dock icon. <strong>Mark as Unread</strong> (⌥⌘U) keeps a session flagged until you open it again;{' '}
        <strong>Mute Session</strong> silences one session.
      </p>

      <h2 id="remote">Remote hosts</h2>
      <p>
        Click <strong>Connect…</strong> in the sidebar and pick a host from your <code>~/.ssh/config</code>, or type{' '}
        <code>user@host[:port]</code>. Swarm connects with your own ssh setup (keys, agent, jump hosts) and offers to
        install <code>swarmd</code> on the host if it isn't there. Hosts can run Linux or macOS.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once connected, Swarm shows the host's sessions, or opens a new session if it has none. Clicking a host in the
        session picker does the same.
      </p>
      <p>
        Remote sessions live on the host. If your laptop sleeps or the network drops, the tabs show that they're
        reconnecting and pick up where they were.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you update Swarm, it updates <code>swarmd</code> on each host the next time it connects. Running sessions
        move to the new version without restarting.
      </p>
      <p>
        On a Linux host, systemd may end your sessions when your last login there ends (for example when your Mac
        sleeps), unless lingering is on. Turn it on for the host in Settings → Hosts, or run{' '}
        <code>loginctl enable-linger</code> on the host once. Many servers (Ubuntu, for one) let only an administrator
        do this over ssh: if you get “Access denied”, ask one to run{' '}
        <code>sudo loginctl enable-linger &lt;your user&gt;</code>.
      </p>
      <h3 id="ports">Ports and forwarding</h3>
      <p>
        When a program in a session listens on a port, such as a development server on 3000, Go to Anything (⌘P) lists
        it. On this Mac, <strong>Open localhost:3000</strong> opens it in your browser. On a remote host,{' '}
        <strong>Open port 3000 from &lt;session&gt;</strong> forwards the port to this Mac over ssh first, then opens{' '}
        <code>http://localhost:3000</code> (or another local port if 3000 is taken).
      </p>
      <p>
        To forward a port before anything listens on it, type the number in Go to Anything (<code>3000</code>,{' '}
        <code>:3000</code> or <code>port 3000</code>) and choose <strong>Forward port 3000 from &lt;host&gt;</strong>.
        Forwards use your own ssh config and keys. If nothing answers on the host yet, Swarm says so and keeps the forward
        open, so you can start the server and reload. Running forwards are listed in Go to Anything as{' '}
        <strong>Stop forwarding</strong>, and they end when Swarm quits.
      </p>

      <h2 id="server-install">Install on a server yourself</h2>
      <p>
        You don't have to: the app installs <code>swarmd</code> when you connect. Install it by hand to set servers up in
        advance (with a script or configuration management) or to use <code>swarmctl</code> on a Linux machine. Both
        builds are static and need no root; pick the one <code>uname -m</code> names.
      </p>
      <pre class="term">mkdir -p ~/.local/share/swarm/bin
curl -fsSL {linuxTarball('x86_64')} | tar -xz -C ~/.local/share/swarm/bin
<span class="d"># ARM servers (aarch64): use swarmd-{VERSION}-linux-aarch64.tar.gz</span>
~/.local/share/swarm/bin/swarmd --version</pre>
      <ul class="files">
        <li>
          <a href={linuxTarball('x86_64')}>swarmd-{VERSION}-linux-x86_64.tar.gz</a>
          <span>SHA-256 <code>{LINUX_SHA256_X86_64}</code></span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={linuxTarball('aarch64')}>swarmd-{VERSION}-linux-aarch64.tar.gz</a>
          <span>SHA-256 <code>{LINUX_SHA256_AARCH64}</code></span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each archive holds <code>swarmd</code>, <code>swarm-shim</code> and <code>swarmctl</code>. That folder is where the
        app looks first and keeps up to date, so a server set up this way updates like any other. To use{' '}
        <code>swarmctl</code> there, add the folder to your <code>PATH</code>. To start the daemon at login instead of
        on first use, run <code>swarmctl daemon install-service</code> (a systemd user unit). For a macOS server, connect
        from the app: it installs its own macOS build.
      </p>

      <h2 id="scrollback">Scrollback</h2>
      <p>
        Each session keeps its last 16 MB of output in memory, and all sessions together are capped at 512 MB; the
        idlest sessions are trimmed first. Nothing is written to disk by default.
      </p>
      <p>
        To keep more, turn on <strong>Settings → Daemon → Keep scrollback beyond 16 MB on disk</strong>, or run{' '}
        <code>swarmd spill on</code>. Output that leaves memory is then saved encrypted, up to 256 MB per session by
        default (<code>--limit-mb N</code> changes it). The key exists only in that session's process, and the files are
        deleted when the session ends or the setting is turned off.
      </p>
      <p>
        Find in All Sessions (⇧⌘F) and <code>swarmctl grep</code> search the most recent 64 MB of each session's
        output, what's on disk included. The search runs in each session's own process, so the output never leaves
        the host it's on; only the matching lines do. The app keeps them for five minutes and doesn't log what you
        searched for.
      </p>
      <pre class="term"><span class="p">$</span> swarmctl dump build &gt; build.log      <span class="d"># all kept output, as text</span>
<span class="p">$</span> swarmctl dump build --raw            <span class="d"># with escape codes</span></pre>

      <h2 id="swarmctl">swarmctl</h2>
      <p>
        The command-line client sees the same sessions as the app. Add <code>--host &lt;ssh-host&gt;</code> to any command
        to talk to that host's daemon instead.
      </p>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl ls</code></td><td>List sessions with their state, folder and last activity.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl new -n build</code></td><td>Create a session and attach to it (<code>-d</code> to leave it detached).</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl attach build</code></td><td>Attach by id, id prefix or name. Ctrl-\ detaches.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl rename build ci</code></td><td>Rename a session; the app follows live.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl dump build</code></td><td>Print everything the session has kept.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl info build</code></td><td>Show everything known about a session.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl kill build</code></td><td>Send a signal to the session's process (HUP by default).</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl rm build</code></td><td>End the session if needed and drop it.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 id="scripting">Scripts and agents</h3>
      <p>
        These commands drive a session without attaching to it, so a script or a coding agent can run something in one
        session and read the result. They take the session with <code>-s</code>; run inside a Swarm session, they act on
        that session by default.
      </p>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl send -s build --enter 'make test'</code></td><td>Type text into the session (standard input if no text). Enter is pressed only with <code>--enter</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl send-key -s build ctrl+c</code></td><td>Press keys: <code>enter</code>, <code>tab</code>, <code>esc</code>, <code>up</code>, <code>f5</code>, <code>ctrl+c</code>, <code>alt+b</code>, <code>shift+tab</code>…</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl capture -s build</code></td><td>Print what the screen shows now, full-screen programs included. <code>--unwrap</code> joins wrapped lines, <code>--ansi</code> keeps colors.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl wait -s build --for 10m</code></td><td>Wait for the session's program to exit, and exit with its status.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl new --wait -- make test</code></td><td>Run a command in a new session and exit with its status.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl status</code></td><td>What programs report about themselves in every session: working, blocked (waiting for you), done or error. <code>-s</code> for one session, <code>--clear</code> to mark it seen.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl wait -s agent --until blocked,done</code></td><td>Wait until a program needs you or finishes.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl agents install</code></td><td>Have Claude Code and Codex report when they're working, waiting for you or done (see <a href="#program-status">Program status</a>). <code>status</code> and <code>uninstall</code> too.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl ports</code></td><td>TCP ports that programs in sessions listen on, such as a development server, with the program and whether it listens on localhost only. <code>-s</code> for one session. In the app, Go to Anything (⌘P) offers to open them, forwarding the port first when the session is on another host.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl grep 'disk full'</code></td><td>Find text in every session's output, output kept on disk included: the matching lines, newest first, under their session. Ignores case unless the text has a capital letter (<code>-i</code> and <code>-S</code> choose). <code>-s</code> for one session, <code>--max</code> for how many lines per session (up to 1,000). Exits 1 when nothing matches, 2 when some sessions couldn't be searched (they're named).</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>swarmctl events</code></td><td>Print changes as they happen: sessions created, exited or renamed, status, bells and notifications. One JSON object per line with <code>--json</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        <code>ls</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>new -d</code>, <code>capture</code> and <code>wait</code> print JSON with{' '}
        <code>--json</code>. Exit statuses: 0 done, 1 failed (or <code>wait</code> timed out), 2 bad command line, 3 no
        such session or more than one matches, 4 the daemon couldn't be reached; <code>wait</code> exits with the
        program's own status.
      </p>
      <pre class="term"><span class="p">$</span> swarmctl new -d -n tests -- sh -c 'make test'
<span class="p">$</span> swarmctl wait -s tests || swarmctl capture -s tests</pre>

      <h3 id="program-status">Program status</h3>
      <p>
        Programs can tell Swarm what they're doing with the open Program Status Protocol (OSC 7501):
        working, blocked on you (for a permission, an answer or a login), done or failed, with a one-line message. Swarm
        also counts bells, keeps the last desktop notification a program asked for (OSC 9, 777 and 99), reads progress
        (OSC 9;4) and the exit code of each command your shell reports (OSC 133). A new prompt or the program exiting
        ends "working" and "blocked"; "done" and "error" stay until the session is seen.
      </p>
      <p>
        Claude Code and Codex report this way once they're set up: choose <strong>Swarm → Set Up Coding Agents…</strong>,
        or run <code>swarmctl agents install</code>. It adds hooks to <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code> and a notify
        program to <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code>, after backing up each file, and the app also sets up the hosts
        you're connected to. Sessions then show Claude Code working, waiting for your permission (with its question) and
        done, and Codex finishing a turn. Outside Swarm the agents work as before. <code>swarmctl agents status</code>{' '}
        shows the setup and <code>swarmctl agents uninstall</code> removes it; add <code>--host</code> for a server.
      </p>
      <p>From a script, one line reports a state, with the message in base64:</p>
      <pre class="term">status() &#123; printf '\e]7501;state=%s:msg=%s\e\\' "$1" "$(printf '%s' "$2" | base64 | tr -d '\n')"; &#125;
status working "Syncing photos"
rsync -a ~/Photos backup:/photos &amp;&amp; status done "Photos synced" || status error "rsync failed"</pre>

      <h2 id="daemon">The daemon</h2>
      <p>
        <code>swarmd</code> starts with the app by default. To have sessions survive logout and start the daemon at
        login instead (a launchd agent on macOS, a systemd user unit on Linux):
      </p>
      <pre class="term"><span class="p">$</span> swarmctl daemon install-service</pre>
      <p>
        <code>swarmctl daemon uninstall-service</code> goes back to starting on demand. <code>swarmctl daemon stop</code>{' '}
        stops the daemon without ending any session. When a new version starts, running sessions move to it in place;{' '}
        <code>swarmctl daemon upgrade-shims</code> does that by hand.
      </p>

      <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>
      <p>
        Something not covered here? <a href="/report/">Report an issue</a> and we'll reply by email. To include the
        details that help most, choose <strong>Help → Copy Debug Info</strong> and paste the result: Swarm's version,
        its update settings and the state of the daemon on this Mac and on each connected host, with your home folder
        shown as <code>~</code>.
      </p>
      <dl>
        <dt>macOS says Swarm can't be opened</dt>
        <dd>Versions before 0.1.2 aren't notarized: allow them once in System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Open Anyway, or install the current version.</dd>
        <dt>A host refuses my SSH login</dt>
        <dd>Swarm signs in with SSH keys and never asks for a password itself. If the host accepts passwords, click <strong>Open in Terminal</strong> in the error: a new tab sets your key up, and you type the host's password into ssh there. If it accepts only keys, run <code>ssh-add</code> or ask its administrator to add your public key (<code>~/.ssh/*.pub</code>).</dd>
        <dt>A remote tab keeps reconnecting</dt>
        <dd>Check that <code>ssh &lt;host&gt;</code> works without a password prompt from a terminal; Swarm uses the same configuration.</dd>
        <dt>A session I closed is gone from the tabs</dt>
        <dd>It's detached, not ended. Open it from the <strong>detached</strong> list in the sidebar, or run <code>swarmctl ls</code>.</dd>
      </dl>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .docs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 220px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 56px;
    padding-top: 64px;
    padding-bottom: 96px;
  }

  aside {
    position: sticky;
    top: 92px;
    align-self: start;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
  }

  aside nav {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  aside nav a {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 4px 0;
  }

  aside nav a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  @media (max-width: 799px) {
    .docs {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 24px;
      padding-top: 40px;
    }
    aside {
      position: static;
    }
    aside nav {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 6px 16px;
    }
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 720px;
  }

  .files {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
  }

  .files li {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
  }

  .files span {
    color: var(--ink-3);
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  .files code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    font-size: 12px;
  }

  .prose h1 {
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 26px;
    margin: 56px 0 16px;
    padding-top: 24px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    scroll-margin-top: 80px;
  }

  .prose p,
  .prose li,
  .prose dd {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .prose strong,
  .prose dt {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .prose code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.88em;
    background: var(--accent-soft);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  .prose pre.term {
    margin: 16px 0;
  }

  .prose pre code {
    background: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .prose a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 15px;
    margin: 16px 0;
  }

  td {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding: 10px 12px 10px 0;
